      statement:
        "<p>A payments-adjacent app is migrated from an old CentOS 7 box (where, you discover, SELinux had been quietly set permissive years ago) to a hardened RHEL 9 image with SELinux enforcing, as security policy requires. The app starts, then breaks in three ways: it cannot read its config under <code>/opt/appd/etc/</code> (files rsynced from the old host), cannot bind its metrics port 9464, and cannot execute a helper script it writes to <code>/var/lib/appd/tmp/</code>. The app team's lead has already typed <code>setenforce 0</code> once 'to confirm it's SELinux' and is lobbying to leave it that way: 'it worked for five years like this.'</p><p>Walk the correct workflow: reading AVC denials from the audit log, the <code>audit2why</code> / <code>sealert</code> triage, and the fix hierarchy — restore correct file contexts, flip the right boolean, adjust port types, and only then write a minimal custom module — plus the one denial here that should not be 'fixed' at all because it's the policy correctly catching bad application behavior. Then make the organizational case: why <code>setenforce 0</code> on this host class is malpractice, what permissive-domain and staged-rollout options make enforcement adoptable, and how migrations stop generating this incident.</p>",
