        data:
          "<p>The concrete artifact that ships to all 40 hosts (config-management role, reviewed in git):</p><pre># file contexts — persistent rules, then apply\nsemanage fcontext -a -t etc_t        '/opt/appd/etc(/.*)?'\nsemanage fcontext -a -t appd_log_t   '/opt/appd/log(/.*)?'   # per map\nrestorecon -Rv /opt/appd /var/lib/appd\n\n# port type — check what 9464 needs; bind as its proper class\nsemanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 9464   # or app's own type\n                                              # if a vendor policy\n                                              # defines one\n\n# migration standard fixed at the source:\nrsync -aAX  src/ dst/        # -X preserves xattrs = contexts\n# or: copy then 'restorecon -R' as a mandatory post-step\n\n# monitoring (post-migration, permanent)\n- ship audit.log to the SIEM; alert on new AVC signatures per host\n- weekly report: denials by signature, trending — silence is the\n  steady state; noise is either an attack or a change unreviewed</pre><p>What is deliberately absent: any <code>audit2allow -M appd_fixes</code> module for signatures 1 and 2 — modules that paper over label and port issues are how estates end up with policy nobody can explain at audit time.</p>",
        deepdives:
          "<p><b>1. The denial you don't fix.</b> Signature 3 — the app writes a script into its data directory and executes it — is the policy working. Write+execute in the same directory is the exact primitive attackers want (drop payload, run payload), and the targeted policy denies it for the app's domain on purpose. The wrong move is audit2allow, which would mint an allow rule granting the app domain execute on its own writable type — permanently legalizing the attack pattern to fix a shortcut. Right moves, in order: change the app to ship helpers as packaged, properly-labeled executables (bin_t under the app's tree); if generation is truly dynamic, generate <i>data</i> consumed by a fixed interpreter invocation rather than executable files; and if the vendor insists and the business accepts it, the permit is written as a minimal module with a risk acceptance signed by security — a documented exception, not a silent allow. Knowing which denials are bugs in the world and which are the policy catching a real hazard is the judgment the whole exercise trains.</p><p><b>2. Why setenforce 0 is malpractice here — argued, not asserted.</b> (a) It is host-global: to un-break three file accesses, it removes mandatory containment from every process on a payments-adjacent box — SSH, the app, any future compromise. (b) It is invisible debt: everything keeps 'working', so it survives until the audit or the breach, and 'worked for five years' is survivorship bias — permissive mode logged five years of would-be denials nobody read. (c) It is unnecessary, because scoped alternatives exist: <code>semanage permissive -a appd_t</code> puts <i>one domain</i> in permissive while the rest of the system stays enforcing — the correct time-boxed bridge while fixes land — and permissive-then-read-then-enforce is a supported adoption path, not an end state. The professional framing for the app lead: enforcing isn't the obstacle; the migration that dropped file contexts is the defect, and it's already fixed.</p><p><b>3. Reading tools critically.</b> <code>audit2why</code> classifies causes (missing type enforcement rule vs boolean vs constraint) — trust it for triage. <code>sealert</code> suggests fixes ranked by confidence — read the labels suggestions seriously, treat its 'you could run audit2allow' footer as a loaded gun. <code>audit2allow</code> output must be minimized by a human: it transcribes exactly the observed accesses, so a retry loop during a mislabel window can produce rules granting far more than intended (dontaudited accesses, overly broad classes). House rule: any .te file in the estate must be explainable line-by-line by its owner at review — if nobody can say why a rule exists, it doesn't merge. And <code>chcon</code> vs <code>semanage fcontext</code>: chcon edits the file's label now; only fcontext rules survive a relabel — one-shot chcon fixes are time bombs that autorelabel resets at the worst moment.</p>",
