          "<p>We chose <b>label/boolean/port fixes over a catch-all custom module</b> because the least-powerful mechanism preserves the policy's meaning and audit story; the price is more triage minutes up front, repaid across 40 identical hosts by automation. We chose <b>per-domain permissive as the bridge</b> (time-boxed, ticketed) over host-wide permissive because it converts 'security vs delivery' from a standoff into a schedule; the price is that appd runs contained-but-unenforced for days — logged, monitored, and expiring. We chose <b>pushing back on the write-then-execute pattern</b> over permitting it, accepting friction with the app team, because permitting it converts a policy control into decoration exactly where containment matters most; the fallback is a signed risk acceptance, which at least prices the exception and names its owner. Residual honesty: enforcing SELinux does not make the app secure — it bounds blast radius; the five permissive years mean the app's own behavior has never been security-reviewed, and that review is a follow-up finding, not this incident's scope.</p>",
