      statement:
        "<p>Saturday, 07:40: the database host's <code>/var</code> filesystem hits 100% and writes start failing. The on-call finds the smoking gun missing: <code>df -h</code> says 198 GB of 200 GB used, but <code>du -sh /var</code> can only account for 61 GB. Three weeks ago someone 'freed space' by deleting a 130 GB application log that was — and still is — held open by the app; the space never came back, and steady growth has now finished the job. Meanwhile the same host shows a second rot: <code>/var/lib/app/tmp</code> contains 1.1 million small work files that a batch job never cleans, and inode usage (<code>df -i</code>) is at 94%.</p><p>Walk the whole competency: why deleting an open file frees the name but not the bytes (dirents vs inodes vs open file descriptions), how to find and safely reclaim the space <i>right now</i> without bouncing the database's co-resident app, the truncate-vs-delete discipline, and then the design layer — a log-rotation and disk-hygiene architecture (rotation contracts per app, copytruncate vs signal-based reopen, systemd-journald sizing, inode budgets, alerting on the du/df divergence itself) so this never pages anyone again.</p>",
