          "<p>Reconcile first — the divergence is itself the diagnostic:</p><pre>df: 198 GB used   du: 61 GB visible\n→ ~137 GB held by deleted-but-open files\nlsof +L1 /var → appd pid 2148 holds /var/log/app/app.log (deleted),\n  size 131 GB, plus a handful of small ones ≈ 6 GB. Books balance.</pre><p>Growth forensics for the postmortem: the log grew ~130 GB in ~90 days before the deletion → ~1.4 GB/day from this app alone. The 'freed' 130 GB was an illusion, but visible /var growth continued at ~1.4 GB/day (the same fd kept writing) plus ~0.6 GB/day of other writers:</p><pre>2 GB/day total × 21 days since 'cleanup' ≈ 42 GB new + the hidden 137\n→ arithmetic reproduces today's 198 GB. Nothing mysterious; a ledger.</pre><p>Inode clock: fs has 12.8M inodes, 94% used ≈ 770k free; batch job leaves ~45k files/day → ~17 days to inode-full, which fails writes with ENOSPC <i>while df -h shows free space</i> — the confusing sibling of today's page. Rotation budget: 1.4 GB/day wants daily 2 GB-cap rotation, compress (~10:1 for app logs → ~140 MB/day retained), 14-day retention ≈ 2 GB steady state. The 200 GB volume is fine when the books are kept.</p>"
