        deepdives:
          "<p><b>1. Why the JVM and the cgroup weren't on speaking terms.</b> Modern JVMs are container-aware for <i>defaults</i> (they read cgroup limits for ergonomic heap sizing), but an explicit -Xmx overrides all of it — and heap is only one tenant of the limit. The failure pattern: heap sized to nearly the whole limit, load fills heap, GC runs fine (the JVM thinks it has room), but metaspace + threads + direct buffers push <i>cgroup</i> usage over memory.max — and the process dies with a healthy heap and no OutOfMemoryError in its own logs, which is exactly why the team saw the kills as random. Policy: heap ≤ 60-70% of limit (MaxRAMPercentage against a correct limit), direct-buffer caps set, thread pools bounded, and the same reasoning applies to any runtime with off-heap appetite (Go's GOMEMLIMIT, glibc arenas for C, Python's RSS growth). The container limit is a <i>process-tree</i> budget; every runtime needs telling.</p><p><b>2. Requests vs limits as promises vs caps.</b> Request = the promise scheduling is based on — size it at working-set p99 over a representative window (include the weekly peak), because under-requesting is how sum(usage) exceeds allocatable while the scheduler believes everything fits. Limit = the blast-radius cap — policy per class, not per-team vibes: latency-critical serving gets limit ≈ request (predictability; Guaranteed QoS, best OOM protection), batch gets limit &gt;&gt; request (burst into idle RAM, first against the wall under pressure — which is correct: that's the deal batch signs). The dead-node incident was batch playing by serving rules: a 20 GB limit gap on a 4 GB promise. Overcommit itself isn't sin — it's how utilization gets paid for — but it must be a <i>chosen ratio per node pool</i> with eviction and reservations engineered for it, not an emergent accident of nobody doing the sum.</p><p><b>3. PSI and eviction: making pressure visible before it's lethal.</b> The gap between 'plenty of RAM' and 'OOM kill' is not a cliff — it's minutes of escalating reclaim: cache dropped, refaults climbing (pages re-read that were just evicted), memory.pressure some/full rising, latency degrading from thrash before anything dies. Alert on memory PSI (pod-level for tenants, node-level for the fleet) at thresholds calibrated per class — e.g. node full avg60 &gt; 10% pages a human with minutes of runway; refault rates separate 'cache being polite' from 'working set doesn't fit'. Kubelet eviction is the orderly sibling of OOM — it picks victims by priority and usage-over-request rather than oom_score heuristics — so thresholds must be set where eviction reliably fires <i>before</i> global OOM: eviction-hard headroom above the daemons' reservation, verified by a memory-pressure game day on a canary node, because this ordering is configuration-dependent and 'we believe eviction fires first' is a hypothesis until tested.</p>",
