          "<p>We chose <b>immutable promoted snapshots over per-node vendor-repo pulls</b> because identical content is the precondition for canaries meaning anything — a canary that tested different bytes than wave 3 installs is theater; the price is mirror infrastructure and a promotion step, which also conveniently produces the reproducibility evidence security wants. We chose <b>waves-with-gates over fleet-parallel speed</b> because rollback is mostly fiction (stated in the design, not discovered in an incident); the price is calendar — mitigated by livepatch stopping the exposure clock early, which is precisely the pairing that lets the reboot schedule be sane. We chose <b>fix-forward as the default failure response</b> with targeted downgrade and previous-kernel boot as scoped tools, because full-transaction downgrades are where estates corrupt themselves; the price is that a halted cycle can leave a mixed fleet for a day — bounded by the 24h decision SLA and by halting at small blast radius. We chose to <b>spend political capital shrinking the snowflake segment</b> rather than engineering around it forever; the price is quarterly friction with owners of boxes 'that just work', paid because the NIC incident and the 400-day segment are both artifacts of exactly that deferral. Residual risk named: hardware-class coverage in the canary matrix is only as good as inventory accuracy — hence the weekly discovery-vs-inventory diff as a standing control.</p>",
